Thanks to those Pat O'Brien, Ashraf Bakar,  and once again to Selden E. =
Ball.

Basically there are three steps you can take, of which each has an =
increase prerequisite of knowledge to understand.

1.  Look in /var/adm/messages.   I noted that if the crash means that =
the system could not write to the /var/adm, it will write the messages =
in the /var/adm on reboot. =20

2.  Run the uerf command.  (uerf -R -o full | more ) will give you a =
long and comprehensive list of the /var/adm/binary.errlog   It is =
exactly what I needed.

3.  Look in the /var/adm/crash for the core dump files.  the crash-data =
file should give you the actaul stack trace that caused the dump, =
however it did not have this info for me. =20

Thanks again.

Cian O'Sullivan





